%[text] # Calculate a Closed-Box Loudspeaker
%[text] This example calculates a loudspeaker system with a B&C 21SW152-8 woofer in a closed box: the electrical impedance, the sound pressure level and the effect of the volume of the box. See the class pages of [`comp.ClosedBox`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','closedbox','closedboxdoc.m'))), [`lspsys`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','lspsysdoc.m'))) and [`result`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','resultdoc.m'))) for the model.
%%
%[text] ## Create the loudspeaker system
%[text] Create the `lspsys` object `sys`. It uses the default frequencies from 20 Hz to 20 kHz and the default source voltage of 2.83 V.
sys = lspsys;
%%
%[text] ## Add a closed box
%[text] Give the system a `comp.ClosedBox` with a volume of 200 L, equal to the $ V\_{as} $ of the driver.
sys.Enclosure = comp.ClosedBox;
sys.Enclosure.RearVolume = 200e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Add the driver
%[text] Mount a `comp.Driver` in the box and give it the Thiele-Small parameters and the optional limits from the datasheet (only needed for the maximum sound pressure level) of the B&C 21SW152-8: the nominal and the minimum impedance, the nominal and the continuous power, and the excursions $ X\_{max} $ and $ X\_{var} $.
sys.Enclosure.Driver = comp.Driver;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Re = 6;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qes = 0.38;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qms = 6.4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs = 32;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas = 200e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Znom = 8;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Zmin = 7;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Pnom = 2000;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Pcont = 4000;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Xmax = 15e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Xvar = 16e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Solve the system
%[text] Calculate the result `res`.
res = sys.createResult;
%%
%[text] ## Electrical impedance
%[text] The peak of the impedance lies at the resonance frequency of the driver in the box, above the free-air resonance $ f\_s $ of 32 Hz because the air in the box stiffens the suspension. The closed-box theory of R. H. Small gives this resonance frequency as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ f\_c = f\_s \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{V\_{as}}{V\_r}} $
%[text] with the volume of the box $ V\_r $. The dashed line marks $ f\_c $.
fc = sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs*sqrt(1 + sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas/sys.Enclosure.RearVolume) %[output:7585e2c9]
ax = plot(res,"ElectricalImpedance"); %[output:30666df4]
xline(ax,fc,"--",sprintf("f_c = %.1f Hz",fc)) %[output:30666df4]
title(ax,"Electrical impedance") %[output:30666df4]
%%
%[text] ## Sound pressure level
%[text] The sound pressure level at a distance of `MicRadius`, 1 m by default, for radiation into a half space. Below $ f\_c $ the level falls by 12 dB per octave, as for a second-order high-pass.
ax = plot(res,"SoundPressureLevel"); %[output:9b517470]
title(ax,"Sound pressure level at 1 m") %[output:9b517470]
%%
%[text] ## Maximum sound pressure level
%[text] The highest level at 1 m that the limits of the driver allow, for a sine signal, with the limits that the driver chooses by default: the nominal power `Pnom` at the minimum impedance and the excursion `Xvar`. The grey lines are the levels that the power (dark) and the excursion (light) allow alone; the lower of both sets the maximum, the dashed blue line. In the closed box, the excursion limits the level below about 40 Hz.
[ax,lines] = plot(res,["splPowerLimited","splExcursionLimited","splMax"]); %[output:1184a899]
colors = colororder;
set(lines(1),Color=[0.45 0.45 0.45],LineWidth=1) %[output:1184a899]
set(lines(2),Color=[0.75 0.75 0.75],LineWidth=1) %[output:1184a899]
set(lines(3),LineStyle="--",Color=colors(1,:),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:1184a899]
xlim(ax,[20 1000]) %[output:1184a899]
ylim(ax,[100 150]) %[output:1184a899]
legend(ax,Location="southeast") %[output:1184a899]
title(ax,"Maximum sound pressure level at 1 m") %[output:1184a899]
%%
%[text] ## Effect of the volume of the box
%[text] A smaller box stiffens the suspension more: the low-frequency roll-off moves up and the response around the resonance rises.
volumes = [50 100 200 400]*1e-3;
ax = axes(figure); %[output:65633e70]
hold(ax,"on") %[output:65633e70]
for k = 1:numel(volumes)
    sys.Enclosure.RearVolume = volumes(k);
    [~,line] = plot(ax,sys.createResult,"SoundPressureLevel"); %[output:65633e70]
    line.DisplayName = sprintf("%g L",volumes(k)*1e3);
end
xlim(ax,[20 1000]) %[output:65633e70]
legend(ax,Location="southeast") %[output:65633e70]
title(ax,"Sound pressure level for different box volumes") %[output:65633e70]

